{"className":"mt-6 p-6 bg-orange-50 rounded-2xl flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-start sm:items-center gap-4","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1","children":["Get your personalised ","Cholesterol"," plan"]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-500","children":"This sample plan is generic. MealCoreAI personalises every meal based on your region, cook time, allergies, and food preferences."}]]}],["$","a",null,{"href":"https://mealcoreai.com/app/sign-up","className":"shrink-0 inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-full text-white font-semibold text-sm shadow-lg transition-colors bg-purple-600 hover:bg-purple-700 shadow-purple-200","children":["Get My Personalised ","Cholesterol"," Plan ",["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right h-4 w-4","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"py-16 bg-gray-50","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-3","children":["Why Indian Food Is Ideal for ","Cholesterol"]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 mb-10 max-w-2xl","children":["Your kitchen is already stocked with some of the most clinically researched ingredients for ","cholesterol"," management. Here's what the science says about three of them."]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-10","children":[["$","div","Oats and Beta-Glucan",{"className":"max-w-3xl","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3","children":"Oats and Beta-Glucan"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-2","children":"Oats have one of the most well-established diet-cholesterol links in nutrition science. The soluble fibre in oats — called beta-glucan — forms a thick gel in your gut that physically traps LDL cholesterol and escorts it out before it can be absorbed. You need about 3g of beta-glucan a day to get the effect, which is roughly 1 to 1.5 cups of cooked oats. That's it. One bowl of oats upma for breakfast every day, consistently, for 8-12 weeks shows a measurable LDL reduction of 8-10%."}],["$","a",null,{"href":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10466168/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer","className":"text-sm text-gray-400 italic hover:text-orange-500 transition-colors","children":"Source: FDA Heart Health Claim, Docket 95P-0197; Brown L et al., American Journal of Clinical Nutrition, 1999."}]]}],["$","div","Garlic (Lehsun)",{"className":"max-w-3xl","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3","children":"Garlic (Lehsun)"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-2","children":"Two raw garlic cloves a day. That's the dosage from multiple clinical trials that shows a 9-12% reduction in total cholesterol. Garlic contains allicin, which inhibits the enzyme HMG-CoA reductase — the same enzyme targeted by statin medications, just to a much lesser degree. Raw garlic works better than cooked; heat destroys allicin. Swallow two small cloves whole with water in the morning, or finely mince and add to your sabzi right before serving. It's not glamorous, but the evidence is solid."}],["$","a",null,{"href":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8297871/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer","className":"text-sm text-gray-400 italic hover:text-orange-500 transition-colors","children":"Source: Warshafsky S et al., Annals of Internal Medicine, 1993."}]]}],["$","div","Amla (Indian Gooseberry)",{"className":"max-w-3xl","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3","children":"Amla (Indian Gooseberry)"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-2","children":"A study published in the European Journal of Clinical Nutrition compared 500mg of amla powder twice daily against simvastatin (a common cholesterol medication) in patients with high cholesterol. The amla group showed comparable reductions in LDL and significant increases in HDL over 42 days. Amla is not a supplement in Indian cooking — it's a regular ingredient. Amla pickle with meals, amla juice in the morning, dried amla as a snack. Getting consistent daily amla is more valuable than taking it in a pill."}],["$","a",null,{"href":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3356494/","target":"_blank","rel":"noopener noreferrer","className":"text-sm text-gray-400 italic hover:text-orange-500 transition-colors","children":"Source: Jacob A et al., European Journal of Clinical Nutrition, 1988."}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"py-16 bg-white","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-3","children":["Regional ","Cholesterol"," Meal Plan Variations"]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-500 mb-10 max-w-2xl","children":["Managing ","cholesterol"," through food looks different depending on where in India you cook. Here's how it adapts across three major food traditions."]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3 text-lg","children":"🌴 South Indian"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4","children":["South Indian cooking is built around rice, lentils, and fermented foods, all of which can be adapted for ","cholesterol"," management. Swap white rice for ragi mudde or foxtail millet pongal, keep your sambar and rasam (they're excellent), and lean on pesarattu and dosas for high-protein breakfasts."]}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/meal-plans/cholesterol-weekly-meal-plan-south-indian","className":"text-orange-500 hover:text-orange-600 text-sm font-semibold inline-flex items-center gap-1","children":["See South Indian ","Cholesterol"," plan ",["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right h-3 w-3","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3 text-lg","children":"🌾 North Indian"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4","children":["The roti-dal-sabzi structure of North Indian cooking is one of the most naturally adaptable frameworks for ","cholesterol",". Switch wheat atta to bajra or jowar flour, choose mustard oil or olive oil over vanaspati, and keep portions of dal generous. It's your best protein and fibre source."]}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/meal-plans/cholesterol-weekly-meal-plan-north-indian","className":"text-orange-500 hover:text-orange-600 text-sm font-semibold inline-flex items-center gap-1","children":["See North Indian ","Cholesterol"," plan ",["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right h-3 w-3","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3 text-lg","children":"🎪 Gujarati"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-4","children":["Gujarati food traditions (dhokla, khichdi, thepla, handvo) are naturally portion-controlled and often dal-forward. For ","cholesterol",", the traditional Gujarati thali works well with small adjustments: less jaggery in sabzis, whole grain thepla instead of maida rotla, and moong dal khichdi as a staple dinner."]}],["$","a",null,{"href":"/meal-plans/cholesterol-weekly-meal-plan-gujarati","className":"text-orange-500 hover:text-orange-600 text-sm font-semibold inline-flex items-center gap-1","children":["See Gujarati ","Cholesterol"," plan ",["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right h-3 w-3","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"py-10 bg-gray-50","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-2xl p-6 flex gap-4","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"text-2xl shrink-0","children":"⚕️"}],["$","div",null,{"children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2","children":"When to See a Doctor"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":["Diet is one of the most powerful tools for managing ","cholesterol",", but it works best alongside proper medical care. If you're newly diagnosed, experiencing severe symptoms, considering stopping medication, or your symptoms are worsening despite dietary changes, please consult your doctor or a specialist. MealCoreAI's meal plans are designed to complement medical treatment, not replace it. The nutrition guidance on this page is for educational purposes and does not constitute medical advice."]}]]}]]}]}]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"py-16 bg-white","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-3","children":["Cholesterol"," Diet: Frequently Asked Questions"]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-500 mb-10","children":["Evidence-based answers to the most common questions about ","Cholesterol"," nutrition."]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-3","children":[["$","details","What Indian foods naturally lower LDL cholesterol?",{"className":"border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-5 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors list-none","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900 text-sm sm:text-base","children":"What Indian foods naturally lower LDL cholesterol?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-gray-400 shrink-0 text-xl leading-none select-none","aria-hidden":"true","children":"+"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"px-6 pb-6 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed border-t border-gray-100 pt-4","children":"Several Indian foods have clinically proven LDL-lowering effects. Oats consumed daily (providing at least 3g of beta-glucan) reduce LDL by up to 10%. Amla at therapeutic doses reduces LDL as effectively as statin medications in some studies. Garlic (2 raw cloves daily) reduces total cholesterol by 9-12%. Flaxseeds (2 tablespoons ground daily) reduce LDL through omega-3 and soluble fibre content. Methi seeds soaked overnight and eaten on an empty stomach reduce cholesterol absorption. Walnuts (6 per day) reduce LDL by 7-10% in 6 weeks. Turmeric reduces LDL oxidation — which is arguably more important than LDL levels alone for cardiovascular risk. Including all of these systematically within your daily Indian meals can produce a 15-25% reduction in LDL over 3 months."}]]}],["$","details","Is ghee bad for high cholesterol?",{"className":"border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-5 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors list-none","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900 text-sm sm:text-base","children":"Is ghee bad for high cholesterol?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-gray-400 shrink-0 text-xl leading-none select-none","aria-hidden":"true","children":"+"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"px-6 pb-6 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed border-t border-gray-100 pt-4","children":"Ghee's effect on cholesterol is nuanced and often misunderstood. Ghee is primarily saturated fat (about 64%), which does raise LDL cholesterol in large quantities. However, the butyric acid and fat-soluble vitamins in ghee (A, D, E, K) provide real health benefits. Current evidence suggests that moderate ghee consumption — 1-2 teaspoons per day as part of a meal — does not significantly worsen cholesterol in most people and may be preferable to refined vegetable oils. The problem is that many Indian households use 4-6 teaspoons or more per meal. The key is quantity: small amounts of ghee are acceptable, large amounts are not. Replacing ghee with mustard oil or olive oil for cooking while using a small amount of ghee as a condiment on rotis is a practical compromise."}]]}],["$","details","Which cooking oil is best for high cholesterol in India?",{"className":"border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-5 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors list-none","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900 text-sm sm:text-base","children":"Which cooking oil is best for high cholesterol in India?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-gray-400 shrink-0 text-xl leading-none select-none","aria-hidden":"true","children":"+"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"px-6 pb-6 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed border-t border-gray-100 pt-4","children":"For Indians with high cholesterol, the best cooking oils are those highest in monounsaturated fats (which lower LDL) and omega-3 (which reduce inflammation). Cold-pressed mustard oil is an excellent traditional choice — high in erucic acid and ALA omega-3. Extra virgin olive oil is ideal for salads and low-heat cooking. Rice bran oil has a good balance of MUFA and PUFA and is suitable for Indian cooking temperatures. Groundnut (peanut) oil is acceptable in moderation. Coconut oil and palm oil are high in saturated fat and should be limited. The absolute worst choices are vanaspati (dalda), partially hydrogenated vegetable oil, and refined corn or sunflower oil which are heavily processed and pro-inflammatory."}]]}],["$","details","How quickly can diet change cholesterol levels?",{"className":"border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-5 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors list-none","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900 text-sm sm:text-base","children":"How quickly can diet change cholesterol levels?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-gray-400 shrink-0 text-xl leading-none select-none","aria-hidden":"true","children":"+"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"px-6 pb-6 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed border-t border-gray-100 pt-4","children":"Dietary changes for cholesterol show measurable results within 4-6 weeks if consistently followed, with full effects visible at 3 months. The first 4 weeks typically show a 5-10% LDL reduction from dietary fibre increases alone. Adding oats, flaxseeds, garlic, and amla consistently can produce a 15-20% LDL reduction in 3 months. Eliminating trans fats (vanaspati, packaged snacks) produces a rapid and significant improvement in both LDL and HDL ratios within 2-4 weeks. Triglycerides respond fastest to dietary changes — reducing sugar and refined carbohydrates can drop triglycerides by 30-50% within 4-6 weeks. For people on statin medication, dietary improvements are complementary and allow many patients to maintain good control with lower medication doses — always discuss with your cardiologist."}]]}],["$","details","Can a vegetarian Indian lower cholesterol through diet alone?",{"className":"border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden","children":[["$","summary",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between gap-4 px-6 py-5 cursor-pointer hover:bg-gray-50 transition-colors list-none","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900 text-sm sm:text-base","children":"Can a vegetarian Indian lower cholesterol through diet alone?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-gray-400 shrink-0 text-xl leading-none select-none","aria-hidden":"true","children":"+"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"px-6 pb-6 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed border-t border-gray-100 pt-4","children":"Vegetarian Indians often have an advantage in cholesterol management because they naturally avoid the saturated fat from red meat. However, the common Indian vegetarian diet is often high in ghee, full-fat dairy, refined carbohydrates, and fried snacks — all of which worsen cholesterol profiles. A well-planned vegetarian Indian diet can effectively lower LDL through: daily oats consumption; replacing ghee and refined oil with mustard or olive oil; including flaxseeds and walnuts daily for omega-3s; eating dal at both lunch and dinner; including garlic and turmeric in cooking; substituting white rice with millets and brown rice; and eliminating vanaspati and packaged snacks entirely. Clinical studies show well-planned vegetarian diets achieve LDL reductions of 15-25% — comparable to many medications — when followed consistently for 3-6 months."}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","section",null,{"className":"py-14 bg-orange-50/60","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-7","children":"More Cholesterol Resources"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4","children":[["$","$L3","/meal-plans/cholesterol-weekly-meal-plan-south-indian",{"href":"/meal-plans/cholesterol-weekly-meal-plan-south-indian","className":"group flex flex-col gap-2 p-5 bg-white border border-orange-100 rounded-2xl hover:border-orange-300 hover:shadow-md transition-all","children":["$undefined",["$","span",null,{"className":"text-sm font-medium text-gray-800 leading-snug group-hover:text-orange-700 transition-colors","children":"South Indian cholesterol-friendly weekly meal plan"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex items-center gap-1 text-orange-400 text-xs font-semibold mt-auto pt-1","children":["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","width":24,"height":24,"viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":2,"strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"lucide lucide-arrow-right h-3.5 w-3.5","children":[["$","path","1ays0h",{"d":"M5 12h14"}],["$","path","xquz4c",{"d":"m12 5 7 7-7 7"}],"$undefined"]}]}]]}],["$","$L3","/blog/cholesterol-lowering-indian-foods",{"href":"/blog/cholesterol-lowering-indian-foods","className":"group flex flex-col gap-2 p-5 bg-white border border-orange-100 rounded-2xl hover:border-orange-300 hover:shadow-md transition-all","children":["$undefined",["$","span",null,{"className":"text-sm font-medium text-gray-800 leading-snug group-hover:text-orange-700 transition-colors","children":"20 Indian foods that naturally lower cholesterol"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"flex items-center gap-1 text-orange-400 text-xs font-semibold mt-auto pt-1","children":["$","svg",null,{"xmlns":"h